From the adjacency restrictions: A cannot be followed by B, B cannot be followed by C, and C cannot be followed by A.
- Words ending with A at length n+1 can come from words ending with A or B at length n (since C → A is forbidden). Thus, $a_{n+1} = a_n + b_n$.
- Words ending with B at length n+1 can come from words ending with B or C at length n (since A → B is forbidden). Thus, $b_{n+1} = b_n + c_n$.
- Words ending with C at length n+1 can come from words ending with A or C at length n (since B → C is forbidden). Thus, $c_{n+1} = a_n + c_n$.
These three equations can be written compactly in matrix form as
$$
\begin{bmatrix}
 a_{n+1}\\ b_{n+1}\\ c_{n+1}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
 1 & 1 & 0\\
 0 & 1 & 1\\
 1 & 0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
 a_n\\ b_n\\ c_n
\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Let the sample space be all pairings; its size is \( \dfrac{30!}{2^{15}\cdot 15!} \). The favorable outcomes are those in which Margo and Irma form a pair; their count is \( \dfrac{28!}{2^{14}\cdot 14!} \). Thus the probability is
\[ \frac{\dfrac{28!}{2^{14}\cdot 14!}}{\dfrac{30!}{2^{15}\cdot 15!}} 
= \frac{28!}{2^{14}\cdot 14!}\cdot \frac{2^{15}\cdot 15!}{30!}
= 2\cdot \frac{28!\cdot 15!}{14!\cdot 30!}.
\]
Since \( \dfrac{15!}{14!}=15 \) and \( \dfrac{28!}{30!}=\dfrac{1}{30\cdot 29} \), we have
\[ 2\cdot \frac{28!\cdot 15}{30!} 
= 2\cdot \frac{15}{30\cdot 29}
= \frac{30}{30\cdot 29}
= \frac{1}{29}. \]
Therefore, the probability that Margo is paired with Irma is \( \dfrac{1}{29} \).

